Reversible unimolecular state change
====================================

Two independent reversible unimolecular state changes, A(ser~U) <-> A(ser~P) and
B(thr~U) <-> B(thr~P), plus an A + B association that exists only so the model
can run at all.

This is the only model in the repository that declares a unimolecular state
change with `<->`.  Every other one (VALIDATE_SUITE/michaelis_menten,
VALIDATE_SUITE/unimolecular_reverse, auto_phos, enzyme) writes two separate `->`
reactions instead, which leaves conjBackRxnIndex at -1 and never reaches the
product-matching path in find_which_state_change_reaction().

What it is built to exercise
----------------------------

Two things that a single reversible pair cannot distinguish:

1. The product-matching (reverse) path of find_which_state_change_reaction().
   Reached only when a molecule carries the *product* state of a reversible
   unimolecular state change.

2. Forward and back reaction indices that differ.  backRxns gains one entry per
   reversible forward reaction, in forward order, so the irreversible reaction at
   forward index 1 shifts every later back index down by one:

     forwardRxns[0]  A(site) + B(site) <-> A(site!1).B(site!1)   backRxns[0]
     forwardRxns[1]  A(tag~U) -> A(tag~P)                        (irreversible)
     forwardRxns[2]  A(ser~U) <-> A(ser~P)                       backRxns[1]
     forwardRxns[3]  B(thr~U) <-> B(thr~P)                       backRxns[2]

   The two reversible state changes have forward index 2 and 3 but back index 1
   and 2.  Code that confuses the two lists reads the wrong reaction here; with a
   single reversible pair (forward 0, back 0) the same bug is invisible.

   The `tag` reaction has rate 0 and never fires, so A(tag~P) must stay at 0 for
   the whole run.  It is present only to offset the two index spaces.

Why the association reaction is required
---------------------------------------

set_rMaxLimit() only inspects ReactionType::bimolecular.  With no association,
rMaxLimit stays 0 and SimulVolume::Dimensions divides the box length by it,
giving a negative cell count and an endless "CELL PAIR MAX EXCEEDED" rescale that
never advances.  That is why VALIDATE_SUITE/unimolecular_reverse cannot run, and
it is listed in benchmarks/nerdss_optimized/known_broken.tsv for that reason.

Expected result
---------------

Each state change relaxes to a fraction kf/(kf+kb) in the P state,
1000/(1000+3000) = 0.25, independently of the other and of the association.  With
200 copies each that is 50 in A(ser~P) and 50 in B(thr~P), with binomial spread
sqrt(200*0.25*0.75) = 6.1.

The relaxation time is 1/(kf+kb) = 250 us; the run is 20000 steps of 0.1 us =
2000 us, about 8 relaxation times, so the second half of the trajectory is
equilibrated and can be time-averaged.

Run it with:

    mkdir -p /tmp/unimolrev && cp *.mol *.inp /tmp/unimolrev && cd /tmp/unimolrev
    <path-to>/nerdss -f uni_state_rev.inp -s 20260810

then read DATA/copy_numbers_time.dat.  A build in which the reverse path does not
fire shows P growing monotonically toward 200 instead of settling near 50, because
P becomes absorbing.

benchmarks/nerdss_optimized/regression/unimol_state_change_reversible/check.sh
does this automatically and compares against the predicted 0.25.
